{
  "dom_id": "step-q47793",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{3}{2x}=\\frac{4}{3x-1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ cannot be solutions since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2x} & =\\frac{4}{3x-1} \\\\ 3\\cdot (3x-1) & =(2x)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 9x-3 & =8x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the addition and multiplication principles, as usual:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x-3 & =8x \\\\ 9x-3-9x & =8x-9x \\\\ -3 & =-x \\\\ \\frac{-3}{-1} & =\\frac{-x}{-1} \\\\ 3 & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $x=3$ is different from $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{1}{3},$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is  $x=3.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
